    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["C1"],
      "pins": ["C1.1", "C1.2"],
      "nets": ["Display_Power_Switch", "GND"],
      "observed": "On the EPD_Driver sheet, C1 (one plate on the Display_Power_Switch net, the other on GND, in parallel with C2 at the boost-converter input) is drawn with value 0.1 µF / 16 V.",
      "expected": "The specification's PDI-reference match table places C1 in the 1 µF / 25 V group (only C2 is the G1-specific 1 µF / 6.3 V part). The intended bulk/decoupling cap on the EPD power rail is therefore 1 µF / 25 V.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "C2 default: 1 µF/25 V | C1/C5/C7/C8/C9/C10 = 1 µF/25 V (EPD_Driver.kicad_sch vs. the Pervasive Displays Reference table)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "EPD_Driver sheet: C1 = 0.1uF/16V, C2 = 1uF/6.3V (caps C1 and C2 both tied between Display_Power_Switch and GND)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The boost-converter input decoupling is 10× smaller than intended (and rated 16 V instead of 25 V). During Q1 switching the Display_Power_Switch rail droops more and produces higher ripple/EMI, which can disturb the EPD COG driver's boost control loop and cause display-refresh glitches or brown-out of the panel supply.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build matches the specification on power rails, the FAN53610 topology, the SKW92A/keyboard/microSD/EPD pin mapping, the Q2 load switch, and the boost converter parts (L1=10 µH, R1=0.47 Ω, three SS2040FL, MCH3478), but C1 on the EPD power rail is drawn as 0.1 µF / 16 V instead of the specified 1 µF / 25 V, a value-and-rating deviation that reduces boost-input decoupling."
}

